<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azawagh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Azawak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Azawagh is a dry basin and a fossil river valley in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, mainly in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> with parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. It is grazing land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders and gives its name to a breed of sighthound. Its pastures were controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwellemmeden">Iwellemmeden</a> confederations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azawagh.jpg" alt="Azawagh" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hautala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azawagh_Niger_BMNG.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Azawagh is a structural basin that once carried the Azawagh river, a northern tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> known further south as the Dallol Bosso. The river, some 1,600 kilometres long in prehistoric times, dried up after the Neolithic Subpluvial and left a basin of about 420,000 square kilometres, which geologists call the Iullemmeden Basin. It is bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Hoggar Mountains</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/air-mountains">Aïr Mountains</a> to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas">Adrar des Ifoghas</a> to the west, and is divided from north to south into a Saharan, a Sahelian and a northern Sudanese zone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> word azawaɣ means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human occupation of the Azawagh goes back to 4500 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> were raised there from 3200 BCE; until about 1500 BCE the region also supported waterbuck, hippopotami and elephants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> working at Tekebrine dates to 1600 BCE. As the climate worsened around that time, the region's Sudanese peoples were replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, who built tumuli. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/air-mountains">Aïr Mountains</a> from south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> in the eighth century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded and colonised the region in the early 20th century, and after the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> it was divided between the three states. Droughts in the 1970s and 1980s drove many nomads into villages and towns and helped spark <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebellions: the Front for the Liberation of Aïr and Azaouak and the Front for the Liberation of Tamoust fought the government of Niger, while the Arab Islamic Front of Azawad and other movements opposed the government of Mali. Azawad, the name later used for northern Mali by the National Movement for the Liberation of Azawad, is believed to be an Arabic corruption of Azawagh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> the Azawagh includes the towns of Abalak, Tchin-Tabaraden, Tiliya and In Gal and the village of Tabalaq, beside the region's only lake. Its Nigerien part, about the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, had only about 85,000 inhabitants in 2003, most of them Kel Tamashek, with nomadic Arab tribes whose Hassaniyya-speakers are also called Azawagh Arabs, Wodaabe Fulani and the Bouzou, formerly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> slave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and Zarma have settled there more recently, mainly as officials and traders. The region is the centre of the Iwellemeden Kel Denneg Federation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azawagh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They raided and taxed the Songhai-speaking riverine communities and controlled pastures around Menaka and the <b>Azawagh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iwellemmeden-kel-ataram">Iwellemmeden Kel Ataram</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kel Dinnik settled in the <b>Azawagh</b> and the region of Tahoua in present-day Niger.</blockquote><a href="/state/iwellemmeden-kel-dinnik">Iwellemmeden Kel Dinnik</a></li></ul></section>
